ain():\n    # Main code for non-administrative users goes here...\n    print(\"Running as non-administrative user...\")\n    print(\"Hello, World!\")\n\ndef admin_main():\n    # Main code for administrative users goes here...\n    print(\"Running as administrator...\")\n    print(\"Hello, Admin!\")\n    with open(\"output.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(\"This is the output of the administrative part of the script.\\n\")\n        f.write(\"You can write any output you want here.\\n\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if ctypes.windll.shell32.IsUserAnAdmin():\n        # Run the code as an administrator\n        admin_main()\n    else:\n        # Re-run the script as an administrator\n        if run_as_admin():\n            # Wait for the new process to complete and read its output\n            rc = subprocess.call([\"cmd\", \"/c\", sys.executable, sys.argv[0]])\n            if rc == 0:\n                with open(\"output.txt\", \"r\") as f:\n                    print(f.read())\n            else:\n                print(\"Failed to acquire administrator privileges!\")\n    # End of the script\n```\n\nIn this code, the `admin_main()` function writes its output to a file named `output.txt`. After the administrative part of the script completes, the original instance of Python reads the file and displays its contents on the console.\n\nNote that the `subprocess` module is used to launch a new instance of Python that runs the `admin_main()` function with administrative rights. The `subprocess.call()` function waits for the new process to complete and returns its exit code, which is zero if the process completed successfully.\n\nAlso, please be aware that writing and reading from files can introduce security risks, especially if the file contains sensitive information. You should be careful when designing your script to avoid leaking sensitive information through files or other data sources.", "country": null, "hashed_ip": null, "header": null, "language": "English