oxicity": 8.14320010249503e-05, "sexual_explicit": 0.0008106676978059113, "threat": 0.0002773767046164721, "toxicity": 0.009328220039606094}, {"identity_attack": 0.00016286912432406098, "insult": 0.0034155631437897682, "obscene": 0.001148372539319098, "severe_toxicity": 0.0001223040890181437, "sexual_explicit": 0.0004419411707203835, "threat": 0.00015299809456337243, "toxicity": 0.006191639695316553}], "toxic": false, "redacted": false, "state": "Nova Scotia", "country": "Canada", "hashed_ip": "f48dbd9cd788089d2c80f5ba1cf89095ce6ee8cce65a3f43f5a3cfa453689781", "header": {"accept-language": "en-US,en;q=0.9", "user-agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/112.0.0.0 Safari/537.36 Edg/112.0.1722.34"}}
{"conversation_hash": "f4500727a04f874aee2f2b6b3a77b8f5", "model": "gpt-4-0314", "timestamp": "2023-04-12 22:12:29+00:00", "conversation": [{"content": "Write a long relaxing story about male Comander Shepard during a shore leave on the Citadel, use a writing style similar to the one Stephen King uses in his books ", "country": "Italy", "hashed_ip": "e548378fcdd5396ea44a0ddbd4888107efbb0cbbf5ffac1362ab48a36c9a8607", "header": {"accept-language": "it-IT,it;q=0.9,en-US;q=0.8,en;q=0.7", "user-agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 10; K) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/112.0.0.0 Mobile Safari/537.36"}, "language": "English", "redacted": false, "role": "user", "state": "Province of Florence", "timestamp": null, "toxic": false, "turn_identifier": 132808}, {"content": "Once, when Shepard was a kid growing up on Earth, he'd read a Stephen King novel. The Stand or something like that. It was a gift from his father, a battered paperback that had once belonged to the elder Shepard himself.\n\nCommander Shepard had always been a voracious reader; he devoured novels with the insatiable appetite of a voracious dog for whose hunger, there were never enough scraps. The novel had rattled Shepard’s dreams for months afterward. Vivid, pulsing nightmares of plague and Las Vegas, of Mother Abigail and the Dark Man.\n\nAt the time - he must have been around fifteen, between the tours in the Sol system that his father would take him on - Shepard found the experience unsettling but strangely exhilarating. He never forgot the way the novel made him feel. The genuine terror it instilled, the specter chord it resonated deep within his soul.\n\nEventually, the pages of the book became worn and dog-eared, covered with coffee stains and grease marks. It had traveled with him during the Skillian Blitz and the Battle of Torfan, and onwards through the ranks until it found a place in one of his many storage boxes onboard the Normandy SR1.\n\nNo, it wasn't that he was reminded of Stephen King's books by this particular shore leave on the Citadel, it was more that he felt like one of the story's protagonists sitting there in a sprawling sunlit boulevard packed to the brim with hurrying people, nattering aliens, and whirring drones. He was here because he was exhausted; exhausted on a molecular level, enervated so deeply that only the vast reaches of space could adequately represent it.\n\nBut it wasn't just physical exhaustion that had driven him to seek a moment's respite. It was also emotional depletion, the weight of constantly wrestling with monumental moral decisions weighing heavily on his shoulders.\n\nIt was the endless negation of destiny, the combat of potential disaster, the relentless fight against a cosmos teetering on the edge of oblivion and a civilization developing a pathological death-wish.\n\nSo, seeking refuge, he ventured out to find solace in anonymity.\n\nHe chose a sidewalk café - one of the many that lined the vast corridors of the Citadel - which catered to humans and the more adventurous races. Strong coffee and a considerable array of sandwiches and pastries drew mallgoers from countless parsecs away.\n\nShepard, clad in borrowed camo fatigues and a nondescript cap to help him blend in, leaned back in his chair and inhaled the mingled scents of interstellar cuisine. It was curiously relaxing, he thought; to be swallowed up in the hustle and bustle of the ordinary denizens of this galactic city.\n\nWhen the enormity of the universe became overwhelming, it was comforting to remember that even the tiniest civilizations had their stories, their heroes and villains, their heartaches and keystone cops.\n\nAs he gulped his coffee, Shepard became engrossed in a nearby conversation. It was a group of elcor, bulky elephant-like creatures with slow, deliberate speech, discussing the merits of the latest volus play with palpable, if somber, excitement.\n\n\"Enthusiastic admiration: The playwright manages to capture the essence of her protagonists with such nuance.\"\n\nHow could he not grin at the granular intricacies of life working itself out on this microcosmic level? But even as Shepard acknowledged the ludicrous, almost giddy incongruity in his own feeling of peace, the clock ticked on relentlessly, and Shepard's life continued to accelerate.\n\nThat evening, a show at the theater beckoned. Some dark tale of asari adventure with exotic dancers and whispered dialogue. Shepard arrived a few minutes before curtain call, relishing the anonymity of the city's denizens, continuing his strange new game of one-on-one with the universe.\n\nBut for all his efforts, destiny would not be gainsaid.\n\nThe play, a meandering existential debate barely held together by a plot of interspecies conflict, ended with an unexpected dose of visceral catharsis as the characters, bloody and battered, came together to face the gravest of all challenges: the forces of extinction.\n\nIt wasn't lost on Shepard that the story had been written long before the Reapers had become known.\n\nIt was late at night when he left the theater district, his sojourn drawing to a close. As he nosily prowled the boulevards and alleys, Commander Shepard knew that he had taken an intermission, a brief coffee break from the relentless story of life.\n\nAnd when he returned to the Normandy, it would be with a renewed sense of purpose, a tighter grip on the reins steering his life, and a dog-eared copy of the Stand tucked away safely in the back of his mind. For in the end, it was the stories that brought people together, the stories that reminded us of our purpose in these star-strewn spaces.", "country": null, "hashed_ip": null, "header": null, "language": "English", "redacted": false, "role": "assistant", "state": null, "timestamp": "2023-04-12 22:12:29+00:00", "toxic": false, "turn_identifier": 132808}], "turn": 1, "language": "English", "openai_moderation": [{"categories": {"harassment": false, "harassment/threatening": false, "hara